---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const VersionCheck = `print(_VERSION)`;
const VersionOut = `Lua 5.4`;

const Winget = `winget install DEVCOM.Lua`;

const CheckPath = `lua -v`;
const CheckPathOut = `Lua 5.4.6  Copyright (C) 1994-2023 Lua.org, PUC-Rio`;

const PortableRun = `C:\\Lua\\bin\\lua.exe main.lua`;

const Brew = `brew install lua@5.4`;

const BrewPath = `export PATH="/opt/homebrew/opt/lua@5.4/bin:$PATH"`;

const Apt = `sudo apt update
sudo apt install lua5.4`;

const RunIt = `lua main.lua`;

const Preload = `package.preload["bay"] = function()
  -- everything that would have been in bay.lua
  local bay = {}
  bay.capacity = 6
  local docked = 0
  function bay.dock(count)
    docked = math.min(bay.capacity, docked + count)
    return docked
  end
  function bay.status()
    return docked .. " of " .. bay.capacity
  end
  return bay
end

-- main.lua starts here, unchanged
local bay = require("bay")
print("Capacity:", bay.capacity)`;

const FloatDiff = `print(math.sqrt(2))`;
const FloatDiff54 = `1.4142135623731`;
const FloatDiff55 = `1.4142135623730951`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Setup: Installing Lua and VS Code</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What You Actually Need</h2>

          <p>
            Two things, and the second one matters more than it looks:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              A way to run a file called <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              and see what it prints.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Lua 5.4 specifically.</b> Not 5.5, which
              is newer and prints two things differently. More on that below.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            That is the whole requirement for Unit 1. There are four ways to get
            there and <b>none of them is more correct than the others</b> &mdash;
            pick by what your machine lets you do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Unit 2 is different and needs LÖVE instead.</b> That install is
            covered on Activity 2.1, when you get there. Nothing on this page
            expires when you reach it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="check-first">
          <h2>First, Check What You Already Have</h2>

          <p>
            Do this before installing anything. Lua may already be present, and
            this one line works in every environment on this page &mdash;
            installed or in a browser tab.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={VersionCheck}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <Code
            code={VersionOut}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="What you want to see"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">If it says <CodeSnippet code="Lua 5.4" lang="txt" inline />, you are
            done.</b> Skip to the bottom of this page and start Activity 1.1.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it says <CodeSnippet code="Lua 5.5" lang="txt" inline />, read the
            note on versions below &mdash; you can still work, you just need to
            know about two figures that will not match. If it says{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="Lua 5.1" lang="txt" inline />, that is LuaJIT,
            which is what LÖVE runs; it is fine for most of Unit 1 but is not
            what the outputs here were written against.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="routes">
          <h2>Pick a Route</h2>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Route</th>
                  <th>Use it when</th>
                  <th>Installs anything?</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>1 &mdash; winget</b></td>
                  <td>Windows, and you can install software</td>
                  <td>Yes, one command</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>2 &mdash; portable Windows</b></td>
                  <td>Windows with no admin rights</td>
                  <td>A folder, no installer</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>3 &mdash; macOS</b></td>
                  <td>A Mac</td>
                  <td>Yes, via Homebrew</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>4 &mdash; browser</b></td>
                  <td>Chromebook, locked-down machine, or you just want to start now</td>
                  <td><b class="text-sn-theme">Nothing at all</b></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="route-1">
          <h2>Route 1: Windows, One Command</h2>

          <p>
            Windows ships a package manager called <b>winget</b>. Open{" "}
            <b>Terminal</b> or <b>PowerShell</b> from the Start menu and run:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Winget}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="powershell"
            title="PowerShell"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            That installs <b>Lua 5.4.6</b>, which is exactly the version this
            course was written against, and puts the{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="lua" lang="txt" inline /> command on your path so
            it works from any folder. Close and reopen your terminal afterwards,
            then check:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={CheckPath}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="powershell"
            title="Confirm it worked"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <Code
            code={CheckPathOut}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>If winget refuses because you are not an administrator</b>, that
            is a normal thing to happen on a school machine and not something
            you did wrong. Use Route 2 or Route 4 instead.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="route-2">
          <h2>Route 2: Windows, No Admin Rights</h2>

          <p>
            Same Lua, no installer. Download{" "}
            <b>Lua-5.4.6-win64.zip</b> from the{" "}
            <a
              href="https://github.com/DevelopersCommunity/cmake-lua/releases"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">cmake-lua releases page</a>{" "}
            &mdash; it is the same build winget hands you, just as a folder.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Unzip it somewhere permanent. <CodeSnippet code="C:\Lua" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              is a good choice; your Documents folder works too.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Keep the <CodeSnippet code="bin" lang="txt" inline /> folder
              intact.</b>{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="lua.exe" lang="txt" inline /> needs{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="lua54.dll" lang="txt" inline /> sitting next to
              it. Copying just the <CodeSnippet code=".exe" lang="txt" inline /> out
              on its own will not run.
            </li>
            <li>
              Either type the full path every time, or add the folder to your
              path once &mdash; see below.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <Code
            code={PortableRun}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="powershell"
            title="Running it without touching your path"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <h4>Adding it to your path, without admin</h4>

          <p>
            Search the Start menu for <b>environment variables</b> and choose{" "}
            <b>Edit environment variables for your account</b> &mdash; the{" "}
            <i>for your account</i> one. That version edits only your own
            settings and <b>does not need an administrator</b>. Select{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="Path" lang="txt" inline />, choose Edit, add{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="C:\Lua\bin" lang="txt" inline />, then reopen your
            terminal.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that, plain <CodeSnippet code="lua main.lua" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
            works and you can ignore the long path for the rest of the course.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="route-3">
          <h2>Route 3: macOS</h2>

          <p>
            With <a href="https://brew.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Homebrew</a>{" "}
            installed:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Brew}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="bash"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Ask for <CodeSnippet code="lua@5.4" lang="txt" inline />, not
            plain <CodeSnippet code="lua" lang="txt" inline />.</b> Plain{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="brew install lua" lang="txt" inline /> now gives
            you Lua 5.5, which is the version with the two differences described
            below.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one annoyance and it is worth knowing about rather than
            being surprised by: <b>Homebrew does not put this one on your
            path</b>, because it keeps older versions separate on purpose. Add
            it yourself:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={BrewPath}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="bash"
            title="Add to ~/.zshrc, then open a new terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Then check with <CodeSnippet code="lua -v" lang="txt" inline />. If it
            still reports 5.5, the line above is not being read &mdash; make sure
            you added it to <CodeSnippet code="~/.zshrc" lang="txt" inline /> and
            opened a <i>new</i> terminal window.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="route-4">
          <h2>Route 4: Nothing Installed At All</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is the right answer on a Chromebook</b>,
            on a school machine that will not let you install anything, and any
            time you would rather start writing Lua in the next thirty seconds
            than read installation instructions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open <a href="https://onecompiler.com/lua" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">onecompiler.com/lua</a>,
            delete the sample code, and type yours. It runs <b>Lua 5.4</b>, it has
            an input box for the few activities that read from you, and there is
            nothing to set up.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Run the version check first anyway.</b> Any online tool can change
            what it runs without telling you, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="print(_VERSION)" lang="txt" inline /> takes one
            second.
          </p>

          <h4>The one place this route needs a workaround</h4>

          <p>
            <b>Activity 1.12 uses two files</b> &mdash; a module called{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="bay.lua" lang="txt" inline /> and a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> that pulls it in
            with <CodeSnippet code='require("bay")' lang="txt" inline />. A
            single-box editor has nowhere to put the second file.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can still do the activity, and the trick is real Lua rather than
            a workaround that dodges the lesson. Paste the module into a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="package.preload" lang="txt" inline /> entry above
            your main code, and <CodeSnippet code="require" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
            finds it there instead of on disk:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Preload}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="Activity 1.12 in a single file"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            The output is identical to the two-file version, and the activity's
            point survives: the module is still a table you build privately and
            return, and <CodeSnippet code="docked" lang="txt" inline /> is still
            invisible from outside it.
          </p>

          <h4>Chromebook, if you have Linux turned on</h4>

          <p>
            Chromebooks can run a real Linux terminal. If yours has it enabled,
            you get the full experience instead:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Apt}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="bash"
            title="Linux terminal on ChromeOS"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="versions">
          <h2>Why 5.4 and Not 5.5</h2>

          <p>
            Lua 5.5 runs everything in this course correctly. It just{" "}
            <b>prints two things differently</b>, and both appear in figures you
            are meant to compare your own output against. Knowing about them
            costs less than chasing a bug that is not there.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={FloatDiff}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Version</th>
                  <th>Prints</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>5.4</b> &mdash; what Activity 1.4 shows</td>
                  <td><CodeSnippet code={FloatDiff54} lang="txt" inline /></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>5.5</td>
                  <td><CodeSnippet code={FloatDiff55} lang="txt" inline /></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The other one is in <b>Activity 1.6</b>, where a loop counts{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="0" lang="txt" inline /> to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1" lang="txt" inline /> in steps of{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="0.25" lang="txt" inline />. Lua 5.4 prints the
            ends as <CodeSnippet code="0" lang="txt" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1" lang="txt" inline />; 5.5 prints{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="0.0" lang="txt" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1.0" lang="txt" inline />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Every other number in the course is identical on both.</b> Those
            two are the whole list, so if something else does not match, it is
            worth reading your code rather than blaming your Lua.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="editor">
          <h2>An Editor, If You Installed Lua</h2>

          <p>
            Routes 1 to 3 leave you needing somewhere to write files. Any editor
            works &mdash; the rest of the course assumes{" "}
            <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VS Code</a>{" "}
            because it has a terminal built in, which saves flipping between
            windows every time you run something.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Install VS Code and open it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>File &rarr; Open Folder</b>, and make a new folder for this
              course. Everything you write lives in there.
            </li>
            <li>
              Open the terminal with{" "}
              <b>Terminal &rarr; New Terminal</b>, or{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="Ctrl+`" lang="txt" inline /> &mdash; that is the
              backtick key, above Tab. It opens already pointed at your folder.
            </li>
            <li>
              Make a file called{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="main.lua" lang="txt" inline /> and run it:
            </li>
          </ol>

          <Code
            code={RunIt}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="bash"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>The Lua extension by sumneko is worth adding</b> for highlighting
            and error squiggles. It does not include Lua itself, so it is not a
            substitute for any route above.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>You Are Set Up When</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              A file with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code='print(_VERSION)' lang="txt" inline /> in it runs
              and prints a version.
            </li>
            <li>You know which version that is, so the figures make sense.</li>
            <li>You know where you are going to keep your files.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            That is enough. <b>Activity 1.1 starts with one line of code</b> and
            builds from there.
          </p>

          <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
            <a class="btn btn-outline-light" href="/courses/lua-programming/">
              Course Index
            </a>
            <a
              class="btn btn-outline-primary"
              href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-1"
            >
              Next: Meet Lua <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
